WEBVTT

legitimacy-03-1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.119
Previously: the production line's second
station first divides people into “us”

legitimacy-03-2
00:00:06.119 --> 00:00:06.790
and “them.”

legitimacy-03-3
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:12.861
This time, we question that station:
without “them,” would there still be an

legitimacy-03-4
00:00:12.861 --> 00:00:13.260
“us”?

legitimacy-03-5
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.980
Episode 3: No “Us” Without “Them”?

legitimacy-03-6
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:26.377
Suppose it is nine in the morning at a
school sports day. Students from one year

legitimacy-03-7
00:00:26.377 --> 00:00:29.690
group stand in the field, chatting in
small groups.

legitimacy-03-8
00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:36.141
The teacher draws lots and gives everyone
a headband. Red or blue. Entirely at

legitimacy-03-9
00:00:36.141 --> 00:00:36.720
random.

legitimacy-03-10
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.050
“Red team will win!”

legitimacy-03-11
00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.870
“Go, blue team!”

legitimacy-03-12
00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:46.647
Half an hour ago, they were simply
classmates. Now his desk mate is on the

legitimacy-03-13
00:00:46.647 --> 00:00:49.760
other side. He scores a basket, and
people here start booing.

legitimacy-03-14
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.603
Nothing has changed except a headband.
Yet “us” and “them” have already

legitimacy-03-15
00:00:56.603 --> 00:00:57.470
appeared.

legitimacy-03-16
00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:07.597
Choose one: if you were randomly put on
the red team, which team would you rather

legitimacy-03-17
00:01:07.597 --> 00:01:08.350
see win?

legitimacy-03-18
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:13.179
In a famous psychology experiment,
strangers were split into two groups by

legitimacy-03-19
00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:18.009
an arbitrary criterion. Even when the
grouping meant nothing, they favored

legitimacy-03-20
00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:20.750
“their own group” when allocating
rewards.

legitimacy-03-21
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.447
Additional example (not in the thesis):
Tajfel, Billig, Bundy &amp; Flament (1971),

legitimacy-03-22
00:01:23.447 --> 00:01:26.041
“Social categorization and intergroup
behaviour,” European Journal of Social

legitimacy-03-23
00:01:26.041 --> 00:01:28.430
Psychology 1: 149–177. The minimal group
experiment; paraphrased here.

legitimacy-03-24
00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:35.584
The thesis calls this the logic of
“us/the Other”: “we” can only be fully

legitimacy-03-25
00:01:35.584 --> 00:01:38.570
constituted in opposition to “them.”

legitimacy-03-26
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:45.126
Drawing this line does two things at
once: it brings some people in and gives

legitimacy-03-27
00:01:45.126 --> 00:01:47.510
a reason to keep others out.

legitimacy-03-28
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.752
Author's thesis §2.3: subjectivity is
produced through repeated boundaries

legitimacy-03-29
00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:52.266
(Butler, 1997); the nation as “we” stands
against a constructed Other (Said, 1978);

legitimacy-03-30
00:01:52.266 --> 00:01:54.356
political identity always needs
opposition, and “we” becomes complete

legitimacy-03-31
00:01:54.356 --> 00:01:55.810
only against “them” (paraphrasing Mouffe,
2005).

legitimacy-03-32
00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:03.270
Back to episode one's three pillars:
nation, state, and sovereignty.

legitimacy-03-33
00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.655
The thesis argues that this closed loop
needs an “Other”: internal, such as

legitimacy-03-34
00:02:08.655 --> 00:02:11.670
minorities; or external, such as an
enemy.

legitimacy-03-35
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.920
“They threaten us, so we must exist.” A
perceived threat becomes the reason to

legitimacy-03-36
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.690
hold the three pillars together and deem
them necessary.

legitimacy-03-37
00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.060
Author's thesis §2.2 (citing Walker,
1993): the loop must construct an

legitimacy-03-38
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.702
internal or external “Other” to define
and defend the boundaries of “us.” §3.1

legitimacy-03-39
00:02:25.702 --> 00:02:28.140
sums up the mechanism as “We must exist
because the Other threatens us.”

legitimacy-03-40
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.843
A counterexample: not every “we” depends
on an enemy. A choir can feel a strong

legitimacy-03-41
00:02:34.843 --> 00:02:38.810
“we” without an opponent—through doing
something together.

legitimacy-03-42
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:44.417
But the nation's “we” is different:
binding millions of strangers together

legitimacy-03-43
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:47.220
makes it hard to do without a “them.”

legitimacy-03-44
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:54.730
Where can a “we” without enemies come
from? We will return to that in the final

legitimacy-03-45
00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:55.490
episode.

legitimacy-03-46
00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.430
This episode's reference card (take a
screenshot)

legitimacy-03-47
00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.730
Us/the Other | Red team and blue team |
“We” is constituted through opposition.

legitimacy-03-48
00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.240
Boundary | A headband, a white line |
Brings people in and keeps others out.

legitimacy-03-49
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:09.831
The Other justifies the closed loop |
“They threaten us” | Threat makes the

legitimacy-03-50
00:03:09.831 --> 00:03:11.220
three pillars seem necessary.

legitimacy-03-51
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:17.330
Counterexample | A choir | Doing things
together can also create a “we.”

legitimacy-03-52
00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.540
To have an “us,” a line must first be
drawn.

legitimacy-03-53
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:27.680
And on the other side, there must always
be a “them.”

legitimacy-03-54
00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.340
Next episode:

legitimacy-03-55
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:36.420
How do five forces loosen this production
line?

legitimacy-03-56
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.620
